# 12. Leave Without Slamming the Door

You can learn most of what you need to know about a man and his parents at their front door.

One man walks in without knocking. He has gray in his beard and a mortgage across town, and he's in the refrigerator before anyone has said hello, his wife still out on the porch. Another man hasn't stood on that porch in years. He can quote you the fight that ended it, and he'd tell you he's free of the place, and he isn't. A third man may still have a key on his ring, but he knocks and waits, and when the door opens he says, "Hey, Dad."

The third man left home. The other two are still standing in the doorway, one with his hand on the knob and one with his back turned, and neither one knows it. God's design for a son is the knock.

:::margin
Genesis 2:24 (NIV)
"That is why a man leaves his father and mother and is united to his wife, and they become one flesh."
:::

A man leaves. That's God talking, not a teenager with a duffel bag, and it comes before "is united to his wife," so a single man has to leave too. The same God put this in the Ten Commandments, where nobody could miss it.

:::margin
Exodus 20:12 (NIV)
"Honor your father and your mother, so that you may live long in the land the LORD your God is giving you."
:::

Leave, and honor, both at once. The man who slammed the door left and failed to honor. The man with his hand on the knob has honored and failed to leave. God is after the man who does both in one motion, and that's what a knock is. It says, I don't live here anymore. It also says, this is your house, and I'm glad to be let in.

## The man who never left

Start with the man whose hand is on the knob. He doesn't think this is about him.

If your mother's opinion still outranks your wife's in your own house, you haven't left. If your father can make you feel like a boy with one comment, and you arrange your decisions to keep that comment from coming, you haven't left. If you're single, well into your thirties, and every major decision still gets cleared with your parents, you haven't left. You moved out. That isn't the same thing.

I'm not telling you to stop listening; a wise man takes counsel his whole life. I'm telling you that a life that orbits somebody else's kitchen never gets a center of its own. Your children need a father who's actually the father, not a grown boy relaying instructions from the grandparents.

Leaving well isn't a fight. You decide and then tell them, instead of asking and then deciding. You say, "I love you, and we're going to do it this way." You stand between your wife and your mother's criticism even when your mother happens to be right. Soft voice, steady spine.

## The man who slammed it

Now the other man. Some of you left houses no boy should have had to live in, and I won't tell you that you should have stayed, or send you back to a man who's still dangerous. But I've watched too many men to keep quiet about what a slammed door does.

It doesn't stay behind you. The man who cut his father off in fury has his father in every room of his life, because fury is a kind of attention, and your life organizes itself around whatever you're furious at. He's more bound to his dad than the son who calls every Sunday. He just can't see the rope, because it's made of anger instead of affection.

And it teaches your children how to leave you. Kids watch how their father treats his father; that's the only manual they get. If they see contempt, they put it away for the day you're the old man. If they see him honor a man who didn't earn it, they learn something no sermon could teach.

## Honor when he wasn't honorable

Honor doesn't mean pretending. You don't have to call your father good if he wasn't; Scripture never asks you to lie. Honor is how you treat the office, not how you grade the performance. A soldier salutes the rank when he can't respect the man wearing it, and he isn't a hypocrite.

With a hard father, honor looks like telling the truth about him without mocking him, providing for him in old age even if he never provided for you, and praying for him, even if the whole prayer is, "Father, I don't know how to pray for this man, but You know him."

And it looks like forgiving him. Forgiveness isn't saying it didn't matter; it mattered, and God knows exactly how much. It isn't trust; you can forgive a man and still never leave your children alone with him. It isn't always a relationship; some tables aren't safe. Forgiveness is a man deciding that the debt his father owes him will be settled by God and not by him. He stops collecting, and he stops making his peace wait on an apology.

:::margin
Romans 12:19 (NIV)
"Do not take revenge, my dear friends, but leave room for God's wrath, for it is written: 'It is mine to avenge; I will repay,' says the Lord."
:::

Read "leave room for God's wrath" as a relief, not a threat. You don't have to be the one who makes him pay; that was never your job. Joseph's brothers threw him in a pit and sold him, and years later, holding all the power, he said the clearest thing about forgiveness I know.

:::margin
Genesis 50:20 (NIV)
"You intended to harm me, but God intended it for good to accomplish what is now being done, the saving of many lives."
:::

He named the wrong: you intended to harm me. Then he said what God did with it anyway. Honesty about the harm, trust in what God was doing through it, and no bill presented.

## The knock

If your father is alive and it's safe, there may be a conversation to have. Not every man needs it; some only reopen a wound God is already closing. But a lot of men have stood on that porch for years without knocking, so here is the knock, in four parts.

First, what you needed and didn't get. One sentence, no list. "Dad, I needed to hear you were proud of me, and I never did." He'll hear one sentence, not ten.

Second, what you're grateful for. Even a hard father usually left you something: a work ethic, a roof, one fishing trip. That isn't flattery. It's honor.

Third, what you're going to do. "I'm going to raise my kids differently, and I'm done carrying this against you." A statement, not a threat.

Fourth, the door. "I'd like to know you as a man, if you're willing." Don't make an apology the price of the door staying open. How he answers is his weight, not yours.

Marcus did this with a father who had never said a kind word to him. He'd slammed out of that house as a young man, and when he drove back years later he sat in the truck a long while before he knocked. His father opened the door and said nothing, so Marcus said the four things on the porch and stopped talking. His father didn't apologize. He didn't say he was proud. He looked past Marcus at the field across the road for a long time. Then he said, "You eat yet?" and turned toward the kitchen, leaving the door open behind him. Marcus followed him in. He told me later that supper was the closest thing to an embrace the old man had in him, and he took it, because he'd quit needing more than his father could give.

If your father is dead, or too dangerous to approach, say the four things anyway, to God and to a brother. The words need to leave you even if they can't reach him.

## You'll leave more than once

Genesis says father and mother, and if your mother raised you alone, that includes her; a man's wife comes first in his own house. Men leave more than once, and fail the same two ways every time. The single man is tempted to cut his parents off or to call home before every decision; leaving well is deciding, then telling them with love. The young father leaves again when a baby arrives with grandparents attached, and learns to say, in front of his mother, "We're doing it my wife's way." The divorced man feels the hardest pull, to go home emotionally and make his mother his confidante and his reason not to build a life of his own; she'll let him, and he mustn't. The man in the middle of life, whose father is getting old, is a boy again after every visit; the four-part conversation isn't too late for him. And the older man is on the other side of the door, watching a son who slammed it or drifted. Be the father in Jesus's story, who went out to both his sons. Make the call that doesn't get answered. If there's something you did, say it once: "I wasn't the father you needed, and I'm sorry." And if he knocks, set another plate.

## Knock, and wait

A real man rejects passivity, accepts responsibility, leads courageously, and expects the greater reward, God's reward. The man with his hand on the knob has to reject the passivity that keeps him standing there. The man who slammed the door has to accept responsibility for the honor he still owes. Both have to lead, up the walk, and quit waiting on a father's apology as their reward, because God is holding a better one.

Whatever kind of father he was, he was your father, and how you treat him now is a mark that shows on you. So walk out gently and pull the door shut behind you without a sound. When you come back, knock. And build a house of your own with a door your son can knock on someday.

## Before you turn the page

God never asks a man to lie about his father. He asks him to hand over the debt, salute the office, and walk out gently. May the Father who never left you give you the steadiness to leave without slamming, the grace to knock when you come back, and children who learn from watching you how to leave you well.

